    contentEn: `<h2>Airport Transfer from Hotel Riu Plaza España to Madrid Airport</h2>
<p>Hotel Riu Plaza España occupies the landmark Edificio España, a 1953 skyscraper on Plaza de España that was converted into a 583-room hotel in 2019. A private transfer from the hotel to Adolfo Suárez Madrid–Barajas Airport covers about 20 kilometres, the longest of the central Madrid routes, and takes roughly 30 minutes under normal traffic conditions.</p>
<p>Plaza de España is a major junction where several of the city's key roads meet, which works in your favour on departure day: your driver has a direct, well-signposted route onto the motorway network without needing to cross the historic centre first.</p>
<p>Given the building's height and the volume of guests it holds, allow a little extra time to get from a high floor down through the lobby before your car pickup, in addition to the standard drive-time buffer.</p>
<h2>Planning Your Departure from Plaza de España</h2>
<h3>Buffer Time for Your Flight</h3>
<p>For flights from Terminals 1 to 3, aim to leave the hotel around three hours before boarding once you include the roughly 30-minute drive, check-in, and security screening. Terminal 4 and 4S, used for most long-haul and many Iberia flights, generally calls for closer to three and a half hours total.</p>
<p>Because Edificio España is a large hotel with over 500 rooms, lift wait times during a busy checkout morning can add a few extra minutes that are easy to overlook when planning your own schedule.</p>
<h3>Traffic Patterns Around Plaza de España</h3>
<p>As a major junction connecting Gran Vía, Calle de Bailén, and the roads toward Casa de Campo and the A-6, Plaza de España sees heavy traffic during the morning commute, roughly 7:30 to 9:30am, and again from 6:00 to 8:00pm. Outside those hours, traffic through the square and onward to the airport motorway is generally light.</p>
<p>Weekend mornings tend to be the easiest time to depart, since the square is primarily a tourist and residential area with little office commuter traffic. A chauffeur will typically route from Plaza de España toward the M-30 or directly to the A-2 depending on conditions at the time of pickup.</p>
<h3>The Pickup Point at Edificio España</h3>
<p>Cars can pull up at the hotel's entrance facing Plaza de España, where there is enough space for a private vehicle to wait briefly without obstructing the square's pedestrian areas. Given the building's scale, it is worth confirming the specific entrance with hotel staff, since Edificio España has more than one access point.</p>
<p>Doorstaff assist with luggage and are experienced in coordinating pickups for a hotel of this size, so confirming your booking reference on the way down from your room helps things move smoothly.</p>
<h2>Departing a Converted 1950s Landmark</h2>
<p>Edificio España rises 117 metres over 25 floors and was one of the tallest buildings in Europe when it was completed in 1953. Its 2019 conversion into a hotel added a rooftop pool and sky bar, which means some guests linger for a final view of the city before checkout — worth remembering if you are watching the clock for a flight.</p>
<p>Because the building anchors one corner of Plaza de España, it is also a recognisable, easy meeting point for your driver, with no ambiguity about which entrance or square to head for compared with a smaller side-street hotel.</p>
<h2>Booking Ahead for This Route</h2>
<p>With the longest drive distance of the central Madrid hotels on this route and a high volume of daily guests, booking a private, fixed-price transfer in advance is a straightforward way to remove uncertainty from departure day. Your car is reserved for your flight time specifically, not shared or subject to availability at the hotel taxi stand.</p>
<p>You can request a <a href="/get-a-quote/">fixed-price quote</a> for this route or review general <a href="/airport-transfers/">airport transfer</a> options before you travel, and combine it with a <a href="/city-to-city-transfers/">city-to-city transfer</a> if your trip continues beyond Madrid.</p>
<h3>Getting Down from a High Floor Before Pickup</h3>
<p>With 25 floors and 583 rooms, Edificio España sees a steady flow of lift traffic, particularly around standard 11:00am and noon checkout times. If your flight is scheduled for the early afternoon, it is worth heading down to the lobby a little earlier than you might at a smaller property, simply to avoid a lift queue eating into your buffer.</p>
<p>Your driver will typically message or call once outside, so you can time your descent to arrive in the lobby just as the car pulls up rather than waiting outside for an extended period.</p>
<h2>Navigating the Plaza de España Junction</h2>
<p>Plaza de España functions as one of the busiest road junctions in central Madrid, where traffic from Gran Vía, Calle de Bailén, Calle de la Princesa, and the roads leading toward Casa de Campo and the A-6 motorway all converge around a large public square. Since a major renovation reshaped the plaza into a more pedestrian-friendly space, through-traffic has been channelled onto fewer lanes than in previous decades, which makes the junction more predictable but also means the remaining lanes can fill quickly at peak times.</p>
<p>For a departure from the Riu Plaza España, this junction is essentially unavoidable, since it sits right outside the hotel. The advantage is that once a driver clears the immediate square, the route onto the M-30 ring road or toward the A-6 and A-2 is direct, with no need to cross back through the historic centre.</p>
<h3>How the Junction Behaves Across the Day</h3>
<p>Early mornings, before around 7:30am, see the lightest traffic through Plaza de España, with clear lanes in most directions. The morning peak, roughly 7:30 to 9:30am, brings a steady convergence of commuter traffic from Calle de Bailén and Calle de la Princesa, along with buses and taxis using Gran Vía as it feeds into the square, which can add a few minutes to clearing the junction itself.</p>
<p>Midday and early afternoon are generally calmer, with traffic picking up again from around 6:00 to 8:00pm as the evening commute reverses the morning pattern. Weekend traffic through the square tends to be lighter overall, though it can rise on Sunday afternoons and evenings as visitors head home from Casa de Campo or the Templo de Debod nearby.</p>
<h3>Why Local Knowledge of the Junction Helps</h3>
<p>Because Plaza de España connects so many different routes, a chauffeur familiar with the junction can choose between several approaches to the motorway depending on which is running more freely at the time of your pickup, rather than following a single fixed path. That flexibility is one of the practical advantages of a private transfer over a fixed-route booking, particularly during the two daily peak windows when the junction is at its most congested.</p>`,
